!! 1. Project Status

TiddlyFlashcards is still in beta. The plugin is marked ''EXPERIMENTAL'' in ''Control Panel'' ➡ ''Plugins'', and some of the workflow is still being built and tested.

Expect rough edges. Data formats, field names and settings may change between releases. Keep regular backups of your wiki — cards are stored in ordinary tiddlers, so a backup of the wiki is all you need to recover them.

!! 2. Roadmap

The features below are planned or under exploration. They are listed in rough order of importance, not of delivery.

| !Work | !Status | !Notes |
|Insert images into cards and sync them to Anki |Planned |Images need to survive the trip to Anki. |
|Image occlusion cards |Planned |Depends on image support. |
|Navigate from an Anki card back to its tiddler |Planned |The `Source` field already carries the tiddler title. |
|Cloze deletions inside LaTeX formulas |Planned |Keeps formulas readable instead of showing raw markers. |
|Find and clean up orphaned data tiddlers |Planned |Deleted or renamed notes can leave card data behind. |
|MCP integration and AI-assisted card creation |Exploring |See section 4. |

There are no dates attached to these items. Base functionality comes first.

!! 3. How Cards Are Stored

Each note type is stored as plain JSON, in one data tiddler per source tiddler:

```
$:/Storage/TiddlyFlashcards/DataTiddlers/<identifier>
```

The JSON lives in the data tiddler's `text` field. Each card carries the note type (`modelName`), its version (`modelVersion`), its `fields`, and its suspended state (`status`). The owning tiddler is recorded in the data tiddler's `source` field, which is why relink has to know that `source` holds a title (see 👉 <<readme-link index:"2">>).

Titles inside the JSON itself are updated by `relink-json.js`, a Relink `relinktext` module. It is limited to the tiddlers and key paths configured under `$:/config/TiddlyFlashcards/Relink/` (see 👉 <<readme-link index:"2">>).

To inspect it, use the open-window button in the card list header. It jumps straight to the data tiddler, so you can see exactly what will be exported.

Because the data is JSON and not hidden inside the UI, other tools can read and write it. That is what makes the next section possible.

!! 4. Why AI and MCP Are Feasible

Card data is already structured: a note type, named fields, and a link back to the source tiddler. An MCP server only has to read those tiddlers to know what exists, and write them to propose new cards.

That opens a workflow of its own:

# Read a note in TiddlyWiki.
# Propose candidate question-and-answer pairs.
# Let you review and edit them as normal cards.
# Sync to Anki as usual.

Nothing about this requires changing the storage format or the sync path. It is a matter of building the integration on top of a stable base, once the basic features are finished and tested.

!! 5. Source Layout

For anyone reading or extending the code, the plugin is organised like this:

| !Path | !Contents |
|`models/` |The note types: `TFBasic`, `TFCloze`, `TFType`, `TFPair`, `TFOptionalPair`. |
|`transformers/to_anki/` |TiddlyWiki markup to Anki markup: cloze and LaTeX. |
|`transformers/to_tw/` |The reverse direction, used when reading cards back. |
|`actions/` |State changes: editor input, draft save and discard, card edit/delete/suspend, config save, notifications. |
|`ui/` |Views: card editor, create editor, card view, card list, editor tabs and readme navigation. |
|`ui/buttons/` |Buttons transcluded by tag: card buttons and card list header buttons. |
|`templates/` |Anki export payloads and notification messages. |
|`languages/en-GB/docs/` |The guide you are reading. |

Start with a `models/` tiddler if you want to understand what a card is, and with `transformers/to_anki/` if you want to understand what Anki receives.

!! 6. Feedback and Contributions

The source lives at [[uzvg/TiddlyCrafts|https://github.com/uzvg/TiddlyCrafts]], and issues are welcome at [[uzvg/TiddlyCrafts/issues|https://github.com/uzvg/TiddlyCrafts/issues]].

During beta, the most useful reports are the ones that can be reproduced: what you did, what you expected, and what happened instead. If a card exported incorrectly, include the source tiddler and the data tiddler.

